WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:05.583
A corner of a battery tray: aluminium extrusions on steel crossmembers.

2
00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:07.516
Welding is out here.

3
00:00:08.536 --> 00:00:16.488
Aluminium melts at about 660 degrees, steel only at about 1,500, and where they mix,

4
00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:18.068
brittle crystals form.

5
00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:21.947
So we join them cold: bonding, riveting or clinching.

6
00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:29.329
This tray has 14 metres of structural adhesive and 96 rivets every 30 millimetres.

7
00:00:30.044 --> 00:00:35.222
The adhesive carries the load over the whole surface, damps vibration and seals.

8
00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:39.203
The rivets hold everything while it cures in the oven.

9
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.834
There are three main families: epoxy is stiff and strong,

10
00:00:43.951 --> 00:00:46.935
polyurethane tougher, and acrylic cures fast.

11
00:00:47.689 --> 00:00:51.149
Superglue and MS polymer are usually not structural.

12
00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:56.040
Adhesive is strong in shear: the parts slide along each other,

13
00:00:56.214 --> 00:00:58.478
and the whole bond area carries the load.

14
00:00:59.545 --> 00:01:04.781
An overlap of 25 by 20 millimetres is 500 square millimetres.

15
00:01:05.503 --> 00:01:10.159
At 20 megapascals it carries about 10 kilonewtons: the weight of a tonne,

16
00:01:10.355 --> 00:01:12.178
on a postage stamp of glue.

17
00:01:13.138 --> 00:01:17.156
But adhesive is weak in peel: pull one edge open, like a sticker,

18
00:01:17.376 --> 00:01:19.582
and a thin line carries all the force.

19
00:01:20.875 --> 00:01:23.650
Self-piercing rivets are pressed through the top sheet,

20
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:28.409
and a die makes their hollow leg flare out in the bottom one, without piercing it.

21
00:01:29.137 --> 00:01:31.181
No pre-drilled hole, no heat.

22
00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:36.756
Clinching needs no rivet at all: a punch presses both sheets into a die,

23
00:01:36.964 --> 00:01:38.868
into a button that interlocks.

24
00:01:39.586 --> 00:01:44.323
Usually weaker than a spot weld, but quick, cheap and fine with painted sheet.

25
00:01:45.402 --> 00:01:48.688
Adhesive plus rivets: the adhesive carries the shear,

26
00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:53.447
and a rivet at each end of the bead stops the peel, in a crash for example.

27
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.172
Same steel, high strength: usually welding.

28
00:01:57.895 --> 00:02:01.355
Mixed materials and sealing: adhesive plus rivets.

29
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.598
Must it come apart for service: bolts.

30
00:02:05.911 --> 00:02:08.454
Adhesive only sticks to a clean surface.

31
00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.065
So it starts with surface preparation: degreasing,

32
00:02:12.193 --> 00:02:14.631
sometimes roughening, a primer or plasma.

33
00:02:15.366 --> 00:02:20.777
On oil or a fingerprint, adhesive beads up; on a clean surface it spreads out.

34
00:02:21.804 --> 00:02:24.358
Two-part adhesive is resin plus hardener.

35
00:02:25.098 --> 00:02:28.465
A static mixer, a small tube with spiral elements,

36
00:02:28.593 --> 00:02:30.811
mixes them just before application.

37
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:36.087
Within the open time, here 20 minutes, the parts must be joined.

38
00:02:36.846 --> 00:02:39.029
If the line stops for 40 minutes,

39
00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:42.523
the adhesive cures inside the mixer: purge it first.

40
00:02:43.277 --> 00:02:48.687
It can be handled after a few hours, often reaches full strength only after a day,

41
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.325
and later in the cold.

42
00:02:51.712 --> 00:02:56.008
A robot lays the adhesive as a bead: a line 4 millimetres thick,

43
00:02:56.193 --> 00:02:57.923
3.2 metres per door.

44
00:02:58.635 --> 00:03:02.327
Cross-section times length times density: 52 grams.

45
00:03:03.347 --> 00:03:09.082
A camera looks for gaps, and every shift someone weighs a test bead: 44 grams,

46
00:03:09.291 --> 00:03:10.545
85 percent.

47
00:03:11.294 --> 00:03:15.647
The drum stood cold, the adhesive got thick and the pump dosed by pressure.

48
00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:18.796
So: keep the drum warm, dose by volume.

49
00:03:19.781 --> 00:03:22.347
After a pull test, look at the fracture.

50
00:03:23.075 --> 00:03:27.707
Adhesive on both parts is cohesive failure: the adhesive itself broke,

51
00:03:27.951 --> 00:03:28.938
the bond was good.

52
00:03:29.718 --> 00:03:34.327
One bare side is adhesive failure: nearly always dirt or poor preparation.

53
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.566
If you can only reach one side,

54
00:03:37.728 --> 00:03:40.979
you use a blind rivet: a hollow rivet with a mandrel.

55
00:03:41.686 --> 00:03:45.691
The tool pulls the mandrel back, the far end bulges into a collar,

56
00:03:45.854 --> 00:03:48.060
and at a set force the mandrel snaps.

57
00:03:49.057 --> 00:03:52.459
In a self-piercing rivet sawn open you measure the head height,

58
00:03:52.645 --> 00:03:56.441
how far the head sits above or below the sheet, and the interlock,

59
00:03:56.638 --> 00:04:01.119
how far the leg hooks into the bottom sheet: that mainly decides the strength.

60
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.118
You cannot saw every rivet.

61
00:04:04.852 --> 00:04:09.473
So the tool records the force-displacement curve: press force against punch travel.

62
00:04:10.163 --> 00:04:13.380
If it falls outside the window, the cell stops.

63
00:04:14.388 --> 00:04:18.939
The window for the final force is 36 to 44 kilonewtons.

64
00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:25.487
After a die change the mean creeps up, 0.4 kilonewtons per thousand rivets,

65
00:04:25.661 --> 00:04:28.204
and now stands at 42.8.

66
00:04:29.242 --> 00:04:33.282
Three thousand more rivets and it hits the limit: within one shift.

67
00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:37.063
So check the die, the punch and the sheet batch now.

68
00:04:38.094 --> 00:04:40.742
With clinching you also measure the bottom thickness:

69
00:04:40.858 --> 00:04:43.203
how thin the button is pressed at the bottom.

70
00:04:44.505 --> 00:04:49.776
In multi-material design you first ask: do two metals touch, and does water get in?

71
00:04:50.508 --> 00:04:55.257
Then they form a battery, and the less noble metal dissolves: galvanic corrosion.

72
00:04:56.273 --> 00:05:01.463
In the galvanic series aluminium sits below steel, so the aluminium dissolves,

73
00:05:01.649 --> 00:05:04.969
fastest when a little aluminium touches a lot of steel.

74
00:05:05.719 --> 00:05:10.165
An insulating adhesive layer, coated rivets and a dry design help.

75
00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:14.415
Think of disassembly too, for repair and recycling.

76
00:05:15.141 --> 00:05:19.170
A flow drill screw forms its own hole and thread from one side,

77
00:05:19.355 --> 00:05:20.690
and can come out again.

78
00:05:21.374 --> 00:05:22.639
A bond line cannot.

79
00:05:23.706 --> 00:05:28.257
And the cost: four hundred points per floor, sixty thousand floors a year.

80
00:05:29.019 --> 00:05:32.885
Rivets at six cents: over one point four million euros.

81
00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.718
Screws at fifteen cents: three point six million.

82
00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:40.630
So mostly rivets, and screws only where you must.
